        "chapterId": 257,
        "headingId": 1089,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T13:00:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1074,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 1090,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 153858,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 257,
        "headingId": 1090,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T13:00:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1075,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 1091,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 153859,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 257,
        "headingId": 1091,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These tumours occur in the uterus and at extrauterine sites in metastatic disease.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T13:00:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1076,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 1092,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 153860,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 257,
        "headingId": 1092,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Vaginal bleeding with persistent elevation of serum hCG after primary evacuation of a hydatidiform mole is the most common clinical presentation {3010701; 25318458; 25607129}. Rarely patients present with acute abdomen or haemoperitoneum secondary to uterine perforation, and molar villi may be seen implanted on the pelvic organs such as the ovary, tube, broad ligament, or intestine {28377901}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T13:00:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1077,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 1093,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 153861,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 257,
        "headingId": 1093,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Since hysterectomy is rarely performed for the treatment of invasive moles, the true incidence of this is difficult to estimate. A Slovak study (1993&ndash;2022) reported an incidence of invasive and metastatic hydatidiform moles at 1&nbsp;case per 121&nbsp;253 pregnancies (1&nbsp;case per 86&nbsp;589&nbsp;live births) {38943503}. In the Republic of Korea, a population-based study (2009&ndash;2015) found an incidence rate of 20&nbsp;cases per 100&nbsp;000&nbsp;pregnancies {30809458}. These findings highlight regional variations and underscore the need for comprehensive epidemiological data from other countries.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T13:00:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1078,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 1094,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 153862,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 257,
        "headingId": 1094,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>In the majority of cases, invasive moles develop from complete hydatidiform moles {26535984}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T13:00:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1079,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 1095,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 153863,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 257,
        "headingId": 1095,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T13:00:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1086,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 1102,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 153864,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 257,
        "headingId": 1102,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The lesion consists of invasive molar tissue extending into the myometrium. Hydropic villi may be visible, and uterine perforation may occur.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T13:00:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1080,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 1096,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 153865,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 257,
        "headingId": 1096,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most invasive hydatidiform moles are complete hydatidiform moles, which retain the villous histological characteristics but demonstrate myometrial and/or vascular invasion. Invasive partial moles also occur {25318458;22740971;8737309}. In cases of metastatic mole, molar villi are found in extrauterine locations.</p>\r\n<p>In cases of degenerate invasive hydatidiform mole, where histological features may be compromised due to tissue necrosis or degeneration, p57 immunohistochemistry can aid in diagnosis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T13:00:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1081,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 1097,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 153866,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 257,
        "headingId": 1097,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T13:00:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1082,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 1098,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 153867,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 257,
        "headingId": 1098,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T13:00:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1085,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 1101,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 153868,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 257,
        "headingId": 1101,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> villi with abnormal trophoblastic proliferation within myometrium and/or uterine or extrauterine vessels.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T13:00:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1083,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 1099,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 153869,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 257,
        "headingId": 1099,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>See <em>TNM staging of gestational trophoblastic neoplasms</em> [[Brierley JD, Gospodarowicz MK, Wittekind C, editors. TNM classification of malignant tumours. 8th ed. Oxford (UK): Wiley-Blackwell; 2017.]].</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T13:00:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1084,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 1100,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 153870,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 257,
        "headingId": 1100,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>In the absence of immediate complications (e.g. severe haemorrhage), chemotherapy is highly effective, with a cure rate of &gt;&nbsp;80%, depending on the extent of the disease {19369669; 26535984; 25607129}. Hysterectomy is usually not the first line of treatment in a young patient who desires fertility preservation. Studies have shown that gestational trophoblastic neoplasia occurs more frequently in heterozygous complete hydatidiform moles than in homozygous cases {20208060; 26535984; 31857680; 32404958}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T13:00:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]